as winter hits the picture in hospitals  across the country is the same a health  service in crisis delays in any worse  than ever before a severe shortage of  beds and the number of people waiting  for routine treatment has grown to over  4 million a grim reminder of the media  challenge facing the prime minister as  he pledges to fix the NHS Peter Burke  and his son James bore the brunt of this  surge in demand James who has a learning  disability had to wait almost eight  hours for an ambulance last month after  his catheter come out leaving him in  lots of pain and covered in blood we  just didn't know what had gone on we  didn't know whether the catheter had  moved inside he had ripped him inside  and we just we just hadn't got clue he  was you know I can't describe how I was  feeling  I was so scared what was going to happen  with him South East Coast ambulance  service apologized for the delay and  said staff were in contact to check on  James's condition they pointed to an  8.5% increase in call-outs compared to  last year this overwhelming demand on  health services highlighted in the  latest NHS figures which showed just  eighty 1.4 percent of any patients were  seen within four hours in November the  target is 95 percent almost 90,000  patients waited more than four hours to  be admitted to hospital compared with  just over 54,000 in the same month last  year of these around 1000 patients had  to wait more than 12 hours last year  that number was 258 a rise of over 300  percent  what we've got to see is some real focus  on getting to the heart of the  challenges in the NHS so this is about  how do we tackle demand we know that  many more people are coming through the  door and they are sicker the  Conservative government has promised a  big cash boost for the NHS 50,000 more  nurses and to bring back for nursing  bursaries axed on a Theresa May but some  question how much impact this will have  we really need our politicians to  deliver on the promise  they're making I don't think it's going  to be a quick fix however this is going  to take a long time to make better and I  think promises which imply that it will  be a quick fix are unrealistic frontline  staff in the NHS they're working harder  than ever to see more people but  performance continues to fall and with  the full toll of winter yet to come the  pressure on services is only going to  get worse Charlotte Lomas Sky News
 

